    "c19": {
      "anchor": "summary#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "1. Introducing Spell Spaces",
      "excerpt": "Spell.spaces is a new platform whose goal is to help small to medium-sized group",
      "text": "So I'm realizing that there really are no \"Members\" in e.g. the Admins group, Finance Team, Events Team.  You're either on the team or you are not.  I think this is the same for Events.\n\nOther people might subscribe to hear what's going on with the team, but that should be public data visible on the team \"home page\" (from their public space).",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T00:18:50.215Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "can read anything public",
      "offset": 367,
      "edited": "2026-11-02T00:25:16.580Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-02T00:27:15.993Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed, and it simplifies things.  Member now means one thing:  everyone in the org.  A team or project has only the people on it, who are its Leads, so I took the \"Member of\" column out of the roles table.  An event works the same way, and a volunteer stays a Member of the org rather than joining the event's team.</p>\n<p>Subscribing is in, as you described it:  it adds no access, and what a subscriber hears is the public data on the home page.  The doc doesn't yet say how they hear it;  a notice in their inbox would be the natural way.</p>\n<p>AT Protocol's own \"member list\" keeps its name:  a private space's list holds its Leads, and a public space's holds everyone in the org.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-02T00:26:55.491Z"
      }
    },
